# Visual hierarchy & additive design

## Less is more

Color and typography — especially **ALL CAPS** — are loud. Hierarchy is king. If everything yells at once, nothing guides the user.

- Default to quiet: neutral text, normal case, regular weight
- Reserve bold, color, caps, and large type for what truly needs to stand out
- One primary focal point per region (row, panel, section) — not three competing heroes

## Additive design

Only add the **least** emphasis needed. Each layer of emphasis makes the whole louder, so add sparingly.

**Workflow:**

1. Start flat — equal, low emphasis across related elements
2. Identify what must pull forward (primary action, critical status, active selection)
3. Add emphasis only for those — weight, size, or color, one notch at a time
4. Stop as soon as hierarchy is clear; do not keep decorating

```tsx
// ❌ Everything emphasized
<p className="text-base font-bold uppercase text-brand">Employee Name</p>
<p className="text-sm font-semibold uppercase text-success">$1,300.00</p>

// ✅ Quiet base, one thing forward
<p className="text-sm text-ink">Employee Name</p>
<p className="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">$1,300.00</p>
```

## Color & type

- **Color**: Meaning over decoration — success/danger/warning for status, brand for primary actions, muted for secondary
- **Weight**: `font-semibold` or `font-bold` on one line per block, not every line
- **ALL CAPS**: Inspector and design-system chrome only (`inspector-text`, `ds-heading`) — not body content, labels, or table data
- **Size**: Prefer one size step between levels (e.g. `text-sm` body, `text-base` title) — avoid stacking large + bold + caps + color

## When reviewing UI

Ask: “What is the single most important thing here?” If two elements fight for attention, dial one back before making the other louder.
